1785861959839128	console-dev	itc:v3:AWLmTP-ZOTJklAAAAAAAQe1v-11rVAAAAAEuugI		item.create	bn-2lm	{"description":"# Context and background\nThe prior visual pass was judged too lenient. A strict pixel-art comparison against carts/frog-game-design-inspo.png scored the current game 5.4/10. The visible frog, insects, boss, VFX, title figures, and much scenery bypass the declared sprite atlas and are assembled from runtime primitives, so existing sprite diagnostics mostly judge artwork the player never sees.\n\n# Goal\nBring RIBBIT RECOIL from polished placeholder art to genuinely authored reference-adjacent pixel art, while improving the unified console binary so agents can import, inspect, capture, and review the actual rendered assets.\n\n# Approach\nDeliver deterministic PNG-to-cart authoring, runtime animation evidence, atlas/live-map diagnostics, an Apollo64 art bible, sprite-backed actors and VFX, a richer material kit, and a strict independent visual gate.\n\n# Acceptance criteria\n- Child bones cover tooling, art production, runtime migration, and final validation.\n- The game renders its principal actors and combat effects from authored sprite assets.\n- Representative native captures and animation sequences are generated deterministically.\n- A fresh strict judge scores at least 7.5/10, with 8.5 treated as genuine proximity to the reference.\n- Full tests, deterministic playtests, asset lint, browser acceptance, and offline packaging pass.\n\n# Project value\nThis makes visual quality repeatable for future carts rather than hiding game-specific manual techniques in one Lua file.","kind":"goal","labels":["visual-quality","ribbit-recoil","tooling"],"size":"xl","title":"Reach reference-grade RIBBIT RECOIL pixel art"}	blake3:a3QaBxSBRrQqQK_Iez-tNJBM005aeKGtDCSGSwFJqIs
1785862051312051	console-dev	itc:v3:AWLmTP-ZOTJklAAAAAAAQe1v-11rVAAAAAEuuwI		item.create	bn-204	{"description":"# Context and background\nThe console sprite workflow can dump and poke palette-character rows but cannot exchange PNG assets with external pixel editors or generated concept sheets. This makes high-quality authored art unnecessarily manual.\n\n# Goal\nAdd deterministic palette utilities plus PNG export, quantization preview, and safe PNG import to the unified console binary.\n\n# Implementation approach\nAdd console palette show and quantize commands. Add console sprite export and import commands using the existing sprite target resolver and validated cart rewrite path. Import must support alpha, exact Apollo64 mapping, explicitly requested nearest-palette mapping, color budgets, dry-run, and machine-readable reports.\n\n# Acceptance criteria\n- PNG export round-trips an authored sprite without pixel loss.\n- Exact import rejects non-Apollo colors and wrong dimensions.\n- Explicit quantization reports source colors, chosen indices, error, and changed pixels.\n- No implicit resize or dithering.\n- Transparent pixels map predictably to source color zero.\n- Dry-run never writes; writes preserve unrelated cart text and reparse before replacement.\n- CLI/RPC documentation, help, skill coverage, focused tests, and full just check pass.\n\n# Project value\nCreates a general bridge between professional bitmap workflows and text-native deterministic carts.","kind":"task","labels":["console-agent","sprite-tooling","visual-quality"],"parent":"bn-2lm","size":"m","title":"Add Apollo64 PNG sprite round-trip"}	blake3:ccepxLf3SBMzRRKkjXh8jg9Bj-abtlpGTSnJGBoFsnY
1785862051482454	console-dev	itc:v3:AWLmTP-ZOTJklAAAAAAAQe1v-11rVAAAAAEuvAI		item.create	bn-3k8	{"description":"# Context and background\nCurrent playtests capture single frames. Strict visual review needs temporal evidence for runtime animation, particles, camera context, and procedural drawing, plus a repeatable side-by-side board.\n\n# Goal\nExtend deterministic playtests with cropped frame sequences, GIF/contact-strip output, and a labeled visual review board.\n\n# Implementation approach\nAdd a versioned sequence stage or equivalent capture options that step exact frames and sample at an explicit cadence. Support native-coordinate crops, nearest-neighbor zoom, GIF and strip output, labels containing stage/frame/crop, and an optional untouched reference panel. Reuse Session and playtest stepping rather than creating another runtime.\n\n# Acceptance criteria\n- Same cart, seed, and input produce byte-identical sequence artifacts.\n- Crop bounds and cadence are validated strictly.\n- GIF timing reflects sampled runtime frames.\n- Review boards preserve native screenshots and do not numerically pretend a large reference is pixel-aligned.\n- Existing scenario version remains compatible.\n- Help, command reference, tests, and full just check pass.\n\n# Project value\nLets agents and human reviewers inspect what the player actually sees rather than only static declared assets.","kind":"task","labels":["console-agent","playtest","visual-quality"],"parent":"bn-2lm","size":"m","title":"Add runtime visual sequences and review boards"}	blake3:MsnI7K02k149HMvs9WT5gZ5svHbWu9jwqKURN7IKteU
1785862051652976	console-dev	itc:v3:AWLmTP-ZOTJklAAAAAAAQe1v-11rVAAAAAEuvQI		item.create	bn-2a5	{"description":"# Context and background\nThe 128 by 128 sheet is capacity constrained, but current full-sheet renders do not explain semantic ownership, overlaps, aliases, anchors, or unused regions. Static map tools also miss levels assembled or mutated with mset.\n\n# Goal\nAdd a semantic atlas report and read-only live-map render/capture surfaces.\n\n# Implementation approach\nImplement console sprite atlas with labeled declarations, resolved animation frames, overlap classification, blank allocations, anchors, palette counts, and unused cells. Extend RPC/playtest map capture with an authored or live state selector while preserving authored as the default.\n\n# Acceptance criteria\n- Atlas output distinguishes legal aliases from conflicting ownership.\n- Reports unused capacity and out-of-date or blank declarations.\n- Live-map capture reflects runtime mset state deterministically.\n- Static behavior remains backward compatible.\n- CLI/RPC docs, tests, and full just check pass.\n\n# Project value\nMakes sheet budgeting and procedural/destructible terrain inspectable across every cart.","kind":"task","labels":["console-agent","sprite-tooling","map-tooling"],"parent":"bn-2lm","size":"m","title":"Add semantic sprite atlas and live-map inspection"}	blake3:QOyjhU66vkSXK9knP9O-RjH7f-mVKIud5EKa8hoxOWM
1785862051825301	console-dev	itc:v3:AWLmTP-ZOTJklAAAAAAAQe1v-11rVAAAAAEuvgI		item.create	bn-3pt	{"description":"# Context and background\nThe current art mixes chunky primitives, thin one-pixel lines, flat materials, and inconsistent lighting. A shared production grammar is needed before redrawing assets.\n\n# Goal\nCreate a concise project-local art bible and an explicit allocation plan for hero, enemies, boss parts, terrain, props, and VFX.\n\n# Implementation approach\nDerive semantic ramps from Apollo64, define moon-facing key light, shadow/highlight rules, outline policy, cluster rhythm, native-size readability rules, material families, and limited single-pixel accents. Inventory the currently sparse sheet and budget reusable multipart assets without expanding platform limits.\n\n# Acceptance criteria\n- Art bible includes concrete palette indices and material ramps.\n- Frog, insect, boss, environment, and VFX silhouettes have target native dimensions.\n- Atlas allocation fits 128 by 128 with reserve and intentional reuse.\n- Reference-vs-game examples explain desired hierarchy without promising pixel-for-pixel copying.\n- Plan is directly actionable by the asset bones.\n\n# Project value\nPrevents another collection of individually polished but visually incompatible assets.","kind":"task","labels":["ribbit-recoil","art-direction","visual-quality"],"parent":"bn-2lm","size":"s","title":"Define RIBBIT RECOIL Apollo64 art bible and atlas budget"}	blake3:Sl45G8EH_Ib9zWngzKAxTVIHRRfsLqxJqnX17HJDsDQ
1785862052003476	console-dev	itc:v3:AWLmTP-ZOTJklAAAAAAAQe1v-11rVAAAAAEuvwI		item.create	bn-3le	{"description":"# Context and background\nThe strict judge scored frog craft 4.6 out of 10. The visible frog is a static-eyed procedural oval whose thin limbs disappear, while declared frog sprites are not used by the player render path.\n\n# Goal\nCreate and render a distinctive authored frog atlas with anatomical clarity, expressive poses, visible momentum, and mutation silhouettes.\n\n# Implementation approach\nUse the art bible and PNG bridge to author native-scale idle, run, rise, fall, hop compression, swing, recoil, laser, fire, hurt, and victory poses. Favor reusable overlays and explicit anchors to fit the sheet. Replace draw_frog_pose in gameplay, title, and victory with sprite-backed composition.\n\n# Acceptance criteria\n- Gameplay no longer uses the procedural frog renderer.\n- Major actions have distinct body mechanics, not merely relocated line limbs.\n- Laser and fire alter silhouette as well as color.\n- Sprite strip/onion/GIF/lint and runtime sequence evidence pass.\n- Hitboxes and deterministic traversal remain unchanged or receive explicit regressions.\n- Native 1x readability and packed browser presentation are inspected.\n\n# Project value\nFixes the always-visible asset that currently creates the largest reference-quality gap.","kind":"task","labels":["ribbit-recoil","sprites","hero"],"parent":"bn-2lm","size":"m","title":"Replace procedural frog with authored mutation atlas"}	blake3:7_aQuT2HQqy3Pi5UcMnM-pp0Zb5IpsYttIgSLYJcb7M
1785862052174922	console-dev	itc:v3:AWLmTP-ZOTJklAAAAAAAQe1v-11rVAAAAAEuwAI		item.create	bn-2v6	{"description":"# Context and background\nCommon enemies often read as missiles or abstract marks, and the boss remains a schematic assembly of geometric primitives despite a strong concept.\n\n# Goal\nAuthor unmistakable gnat, beetle, wasp, and multipart Colonel Buzzkill sprites using the same cluster and lighting language as the frog.\n\n# Implementation approach\nCreate readable locomotion and attack silhouettes for common bugs, reusable wing/limb parts where helpful, and a modular boss pod with head, wings, weapons, phase damage, and defeat states. Replace procedural enemy renderers while preserving interaction envelopes.\n\n# Acceptance criteria\n- Every common enemy reads as its insect type at native resolution.\n- Buzzkill phases and vulnerable/damaged states are visually distinct.\n- Runtime bounds remain aligned with the rendered interactive regions.\n- Sprite and runtime sequence evidence pass.\n- Title/victory reuse authored enemy assets instead of scaled primitives.\n- Focused combat, boss, and deterministic tests pass.\n\n# Project value\nRaises both moment-to-moment readability and the visual climax without game-specific engine changes.","kind":"task","labels":["ribbit-recoil","sprites","enemies"],"parent":"bn-2lm","size":"m","title":"Replace procedural insects and Buzzkill with authored sprites"}	blake3:HhJyi_kgxE4k0AMJq3-Sf0Qtz-Lpo7viNi4--jkHa7U
1785862052346792	console-dev	itc:v3:AWLmTP-ZOTJklAAAAAAAQe1v-11rVAAAAAEuwQI		item.create	bn-1vn	{"description":"# Context and background\nThe environment has good coverage but relies on repeated trusses, flat rectangles, and isolated light sources. Static map inspection also understates runtime-built terrain.\n\n# Goal\nBuild a compact reusable environment kit that approaches the reference through material specificity, local lighting, authored irregularity, and controlled depth.\n\n# Implementation approach\nAuthor roughly 20 to 24 terrain tiles and 8 to 10 props covering platform caps, undersides, metal panels, masonry, pipes, vents, fencing, lamps, rooftop debris, signs, and clean/damaged or lit/unlit variants. Propagate moon and lamp lighting into rims and nearby surfaces. Replace the most conspicuous procedural/repeated geometry.\n\n# Acceptance criteria\n- Major playtest zones use visibly distinct materials and landmarks.\n- Tile seams and repetition are inspected at native and enlarged scale.\n- Foreground, midground, and background remain hierarchically separated.\n- Local lights visibly affect adjacent architecture.\n- Runtime/live-map and review-board evidence cover every zone.\n- Collision and deterministic routes remain green.\n\n# Project value\nTurns the level from a schematic stage into an authored place while creating a reusable kit for later levels.","kind":"task","labels":["ribbit-recoil","environment","pixel-art"],"parent":"bn-2lm","size":"m","title":"Build authored rooftop material and prop kit"}	blake3:3eXdMOr8ERkL4oOYom_YwLgOxbuJzNMapTo4CU_R0hc
1785862052515923	console-dev	itc:v3:AWLmTP-ZOTJklAAAAAAAQe1v-11rVAAAAAEuwgI		item.create	bn-f9y	{"description":"# Context and background\nLaser, fire, tongue impacts, and boss explosions are readable but made from overlapping bars, circles, and crossing lines. They obscure actors and lack staged pixel-art shapes.\n\n# Goal\nCreate authored, directional VFX strips whose anticipation, contact, expansion, debris, and smoke stages remain legible around combatants.\n\n# Implementation approach\nAuthor tongue latch/contact, laser muzzle/core/impact, tapered fire plume, hit sparks, explosion-to-smoke, and boss-destruction components. Use sparse procedural particles only as secondary accents and preserve dark separation around actors.\n\n# Acceptance criteria\n- Core combat effects are sprite-backed and temporally staged.\n- Laser has distinct origin, core, and impact.\n- Fire has a directional taper and readable target contact.\n- Explosions preserve actor/boss silhouettes and transition cleanly to smoke.\n- Runtime GIF/strip and representative playtest captures pass strict inspection.\n- Gameplay timing, hit-stop, shake, and damage behavior remain unchanged.\n\n# Project value\nRaises the weakest and noisiest action frames while establishing reusable effects for later levels.","kind":"task","labels":["ribbit-recoil","vfx","sprites"],"parent":"bn-2lm","size":"m","title":"Replace procedural mutation and combat VFX with sprite strips"}	blake3:YccIm_cqg02MIF387LAvD6pHKaDKuVxZaYr5MffloCM
1785862052683523	console-dev	itc:v3:AWLmTP-ZOTJklAAAAAAAQe1v-11rVAAAAAEuwwI		item.create	bn-240	{"description":"# Context and background\nRuntime screenshots prove final pixels but cannot explain which primitives or sprite calls produced them. This is especially limiting when art mixes map, sprite, procedural, particle, and HUD layers.\n\n# Goal\nAdd deterministic read-only draw-event tracing suitable for proving sprite adoption, inspecting bounds, and isolating visual systems.\n\n# Implementation approach\nRecord bounded draw events with operation type, resolved screen/world bounds, camera, clip, palette state, sprite or animation identity, and optional semantic tags. Expose JSON through run, RPC, and playtest without changing rendered output.\n\n# Acceptance criteria\n- Tracing is opt-in and produces byte-identical framebuffers when enabled or disabled.\n- Event buffers are bounded and stable.\n- Sprite-backed and primitive-backed paths are distinguishable.\n- Camera and clipping are reflected accurately.\n- Help, RPC docs, focused tests, wasm parity, and full just check pass.\n\n# Project value\nProvides a general forensic visual diagnostic without embedding an AI judge or game-specific layer model.","kind":"task","labels":["console-core","console-agent","diagnostics"],"parent":"bn-2lm","size":"m","title":"Add optional draw-event tracing for visual isolation"}	blake3:8fCFOMVkhotZG6IDAgjvq7D_XTixbbM478b1jeLC9bc
1785862052848370	console-dev	itc:v3:AWLmTP-ZOTJklAAAAAAAQe1v-11rVAAAAAEuxAI		item.create	bn-3ix	{"description":"# Context and background\nA deliberately strict judge established a 5.4 out of 10 baseline. Earlier permissive scoring hid large gaps in anatomy, materials, lighting, and cluster discipline.\n\n# Goal\nValidate the completed authored-art migration against the supplied reference and ship only after a materially stricter quality gate.\n\n# Implementation approach\nGenerate deterministic native screenshots, sprite strips, runtime GIFs, and a labeled review board. Ask a fresh judge to ignore gameplay and score sprite craft/reference fidelity with 5 as placeholder, 7 as solid indie art, 8.5 as genuine proximity, and 9 as professional parity. Address actionable visual blockers and repeat.\n\n# Acceptance criteria\n- Fresh strict overall score is at least 7.5, with no category below 7.\n- No declared principal sprite is unused by its intended render path.\n- Full just check, sprite/map/music lint, deterministic scenarios, browser acceptance, and offline pack pass.\n- Final evidence and score are recorded on the goal.\n- Remaining gap to 8.5 is documented honestly if parity is not reached.\n\n# Project value\nReplaces subjective optimism with a reproducible, evidence-backed visual release gate.","kind":"task","labels":["ribbit-recoil","visual-validation","release"],"parent":"bn-2lm","size":"s","title":"Pass strict reference-focused visual gate and package"}	blake3:IJxLxJQDJ4rK0auf414x-6UBFOsHv6wKeV3Ncaqb2HE
