1785870510039913	console-dev	itc:v3:AWLmTP-ZOTJklAAAAAAAQe1v-11rVAAAAAEu7gI		item.comment	bn-3le	{"body":"Authored 10-frame image-guided frog atlas and 6 mutation overlays integrated. Strict sprite judge PASS 7.6/10 (all categories >=7; silhouette 7.9, clusters 7.7, mechanics 7.8, mutations 7.6, consistency 7.4, reference fidelity 7.2). Final tongue/snout overlap corrected. Exact atlas source rebuild is byte-identical; cargo ribbit_recoil 10/10, deterministic playtest 61 stages/464 frames, frog.run lint drift 0.39px."}	blake3:zu2kAW34S_1VB_338KArvzR7XmXch3GgkzU2G4nDYOk
1785870679871421	console-dev	itc:v3:AWLmTP-ZOTJklAAAAAAAQe1v-11rVAAAAAEu7wI		item.create	bn-1ao	{"description":"# Context & Background\nCarts are intentionally portable single text files, but using that distribution format as the authoring format forces Lua, sprites, maps, metadata, instruments, SFX, and music into one large file. Agents and humans need normal source files, deterministic generated artifacts, and a single console-native workflow.\n\n# Goal\nMake a project directory with console.toml the editable source of truth and compile it into the existing readable .cart format. The unified console binary remains the only developer executable.\n\n# Design Commitments\n- .cart remains the runtime and distribution format.\n- Builds are deterministic, path-safe, validated, and atomic.\n- Lua modules preserve scope/return/cache semantics rather than raw concatenation.\n- PNG conversion reuses the existing Apollo64 quantization/import implementation and never hides lossy choices.\n- Sprite placements are explicit and stable; automatic atlas repacking is future work.\n- Existing .cart inputs remain accepted by run/playtest/serve/pack.\n\n# Completion\nAll child tasks are complete; console build, run, playtest, serve, and pack work with a project; an end-to-end fixture proves multi-file Lua, separated data, PNG assembly, reproducibility, live refresh, and packaged HTML.","kind":"goal","labels":["build-system","authoring","console-cli"],"size":"xl","title":"Project compiler for multi-file console games"}	blake3:Ox1snPst3S8b9g8xINxbySgkAeStTgfHxFJz0MM5JX0
1785870865148111	console-dev	itc:v3:AWLmTP-ZOTJklAAAAAAAQe1v-11rVAAAAAEu8AI		item.create	bn-2vu	{"description":"# Context\nThe single-file .cart format is a good runtime and distribution artifact but a poor source tree. This task establishes the project manifest and compiler foundation used by every later authoring feature.\n\n# Goal\nAdd console build <project> with a versioned console.toml manifest that assembles a validated .cart from a Lua entry file and separated native section files.\n\n# Approach\n- Discover a project from a directory or console.toml path.\n- Define documented cart metadata, Lua entry/root, raw section files, and output settings.\n- Resolve every input under the project root, reject traversal and symlink escapes, and identify duplicate or reserved sections.\n- Emit canonical section ordering and newline rules so identical sources produce identical bytes.\n- Write outputs atomically; parse the generated bytes through console_core::Cart before publishing.\n- Add --check for CI drift detection and text/pretty/json build reports including inputs, output, and content identity.\n- Preserve existing .cart syntax and validation; this is a compiler into it, not a replacement format.\n\n# Acceptance\n- A fixture project builds successfully through the unified console binary.\n- Raw sprites, map, gfx metadata, instruments, SFX, music, and additional allowed sections can live in separate files.\n- Two builds from the same inputs are byte-identical.\n- Missing inputs, escaping paths, symlink escapes, malformed manifests, section-marker injection, invalid final carts, and stale --check outputs fail with actionable diagnostics.\n- Normal builds do not leave partial outputs on failure.\n- Unit and CLI integration tests cover the manifest and compiler contract.","kind":"task","labels":["build-system","manifest","cart-format"],"parent":"bn-1ao","size":"m","title":"Define console.toml and deterministic cart compiler"}	blake3:Ho3Tz6r1jZltdBd2RQyQY_MMeF4ErjgHVpcLGYNUAvs
1785870865302490	console-dev	itc:v3:AWLmTP-ZOTJklAAAAAAAQe1v-11rVAAAAAEu8QI		item.create	bn-2kr	{"description":"# Context\nMulti-file Lua must preserve module scope, return values, and execute-once behavior. Raw concatenation leaks locals and changes semantics, while exposing the host Lua package loader would violate the console sandbox.\n\n# Goal\nTeach the project compiler to resolve static Lua modules and emit one sandbox-compatible __lua__ section with useful source provenance.\n\n# Approach\n- Treat the manifest Lua entry and root as the only module namespace.\n- Recognize literal require(\"foo.bar\") and require \"foo.bar\" imports and resolve them deterministically to files under the Lua root.\n- Reject dynamic require calls, missing or duplicate modules, namespace/path escapes, and dependency cycles at build time.\n- Compile modules into private closures with a private loader and cache so each executes once, returned values are preserved, and nil follows Lua require behavior.\n- Do not expose package, a global host require, dofile, loadfile, or load at runtime.\n- Produce generated-line to source-file/line provenance in the build report or a documented generated metadata section, and use it in project-aware diagnostics where practical.\n\n# Acceptance\n- Tests prove isolated locals, module return values, single execution, both literal require syntaxes, deterministic graph ordering, and an entry module that uses nested modules.\n- Tests prove clear errors for dynamic imports, missing modules, cycles, duplicate names, and paths outside the project.\n- The generated Lua executes in the existing console sandbox with no package-loader capability added.\n- Build reports let a developer map generated Lua ranges back to source modules.","kind":"task","labels":["build-system","lua","diagnostics"],"parent":"bn-1ao","size":"m","title":"Bundle static Lua modules with source provenance"}	blake3:b2WgnilWzYRagjHxFAw3uioUecSAF57beZXZgFCdc9A
1785870865454157	console-dev	itc:v3:AWLmTP-ZOTJklAAAAAAAQe1v-11rVAAAAAEu8gI		item.create	bn-aiu	{"description":"# Context\nThe console already has deterministic Apollo64 PNG quantization and sprite import/export tools. Project builds should reuse that implementation instead of requiring agents to hand-author sprite hex or duplicate color logic.\n\n# Goal\nAllow console.toml to place named PNG assets explicitly into the 128 by 128 sprite sheet and generate matching graphics metadata.\n\n# Approach\n- Add repeatable manifest sprite assets with a stable name, PNG path, explicit pixel or tile placement, anchor, and conversion policy.\n- Require dimensions compatible with console sprite tiles; reject out-of-bounds and overlapping regions.\n- Reuse the existing exact, nearest, and quantize Apollo64 paths, alpha threshold, and color-budget validation through shared library helpers.\n- Keep lossy choices explicit. Never silently resize or dither, and preserve source index 0 transparency rules.\n- Generate the __sprites__ sheet plus compatible __gfx_meta__ entries while allowing separately authored animations or other metadata to compose without duplicate names.\n- Make results independent of filesystem enumeration order.\n\n# Acceptance\n- A fixture builds multiple differently sized PNGs at explicit stable placements and the runtime resolves their generated sprite names.\n- Exact, nearest, and quantize modes match the existing standalone tools.\n- Transparent pixels, color budgets, invalid dimensions, overlaps, duplicate names, palette failures, and sheet bounds have focused tests and actionable errors.\n- Reordering unrelated manifest entries does not shift placements or change sprite identities.","kind":"task","labels":["build-system","sprites","png"],"parent":"bn-1ao","size":"m","title":"Assemble explicit PNG sprite assets during project builds"}	blake3:TbXuIVP1_HD_U9EZCkCYM5ygOcC7HKiemlIt3r_otE0
1785870865615368	console-dev	itc:v3:AWLmTP-ZOTJklAAAAAAAQe1v-11rVAAAAAEu8wI		item.create	bn-75b	{"description":"# Context\nA project compiler only improves the workflow if developers can point the normal unified CLI at a source project. Requiring a manual intermediate build before every run or serve would make iteration slow and error-prone.\n\n# Goal\nMake console run, playtest, serve, and pack accept either an existing .cart file or a console project.\n\n# Approach\n- Introduce one shared input resolver that distinguishes cart files, console.toml files, and project directories without changing existing cart behavior.\n- Compile projects in memory for run and playtest.\n- Compile projects directly into the single-file HTML artifact for pack.\n- For serve, rebuild and revalidate the project for every GET or HEAD so edits appear on refresh; retain the existing loopback, Host-header, DNS-rebinding, content-length, and no-store protections.\n- Surface compiler/source-provenance diagnostics consistently through all commands.\n- Keep mutating low-level sprite, map, and music commands cart-oriented in the first release unless a safe source-file mapping is explicitly implemented.\n\n# Acceptance\n- Existing .cart invocations remain green.\n- Each target command works with both a project directory and explicit console.toml.\n- serve reflects source changes without restart, never serves stale invalid output as fresh, and retains hostile Host-header regressions.\n- pack embeds exactly the compiled project cart and remains portable.\n- CLI help, text output, and JSON/error behavior document the accepted inputs.","kind":"task","labels":["console-cli","build-system","serve"],"parent":"bn-1ao","size":"m","title":"Make runtime and shipping commands accept projects"}	blake3:gVteKUe2o_LBA6xt5tTHdbiKx4eDTj2QRq_Uh6RfJ3s
1785870865767672	console-dev	itc:v3:AWLmTP-ZOTJklAAAAAAAQe1v-11rVAAAAAEu9AI		item.create	bn-3q5	{"description":"# Context\nThe project workflow needs an executable reference that exercises every source type, plus documentation agents can discover progressively. Determinism and command integration should be protected by repository-level checks.\n\n# Goal\nShip a representative multi-file game project, end-to-end tests, and comprehensive user and skill documentation for the authoring workflow.\n\n# Approach\n- Add a compact example project with nested Lua modules, several PNG sprites, a map, graphics metadata, instruments, SFX, and music.\n- Exercise build, --check, run/playtest validation, live serve rebuilding, and HTML packing in integration tests.\n- Compare repeated build bytes and cover invalid source edits without relying only on unit tests.\n- Document console.toml, module constraints, sprite conversion choices, section-file formats, output behavior, diagnostics, migration from hand-authored carts, and command recipes.\n- Update the repository skill through progressive-disclosure references and its source-backed API/CLI checker so future agents learn console build.\n- Avoid disrupting active game carts or unrelated workspaces.\n\n# Acceptance\n- A new developer can copy the example, edit separate sources, build a cart, play it, serve it, and pack it from documented commands.\n- The example exercises every native cart section and at least one nested module and PNG conversion.\n- Repository checks detect nondeterministic output, stale --check artifacts, broken project-aware commands, and drift between command help/docs/skill references.\n- just check passes and the installed console binary exposes the documented workflow.","kind":"task","labels":["docs","testing","build-system"],"parent":"bn-1ao","size":"m","title":"Ship multi-file example, migration guide, and build gates"}	blake3:EO7xGQXw6wSIatxrl35YiBIPRjdqjG4t5iS_bfUZ1I0
1785870876684531	console-dev	itc:v3:AWLmTP-ZOTJklAAAAAAAQe1v-11rVAAAAAEu9QI		item.link	bn-2kr	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-2vu"}	blake3:CJY7oCacbNnqXHf5eWASGGlqXMk9ONaMMsGzf9zA7Bk
