1785882341788365	console-dev	itc:v3:AWLmTP-ZOTJklAAAAAAAQe1v-11rVAAAAAEumwM		item.create	bn-3rx	{"description":"# Context\nConsole can build projects from named PNG sprite assets and a raw map section, but environment production still has no single authored source that compiles visual layers, metatiles, tile semantics, and object placement together. RIBBIT RECOIL currently spreads this responsibility across a bespoke atlas shell script, raw pixel files, runtime build_level topology substitution, procedural backdrop and architecture drawing, and hand-written capture stages. Its stored cart map is initially empty, so static inspection cannot represent the authored level.\n\nPaintop now provides bounded Lua and exact indexed images, with exact composition tracked separately in Paintop bn-1nd9. Console should consume ordinary PNG and data artifacts through a stable boundary and remain usable with any compliant image producer.\n\n# Outcome\nAn agent can author a layered environment as reviewable files, compile it deterministically into Console project assets and Lua data, see whether it fits the fixed atlas and map budgets, and inspect seams, adjacency, collision meaning, and final runtime placement before hand-writing game rendering code.\n\n# Implementation direction\nDesign a versioned scene or tileset manifest and a console scene compile surface. Inputs should support named Apollo64 PNG layers such as far, mid, play, and foreground; an 8 by 8 semantic grid; named tiles and source rectangles; named metatile grids; object anchors; explicit atlas reservations; deterministic four- or eight-neighbor autotile lookups; weighted variants with an explicit seed; and authored per-cell overrides.\n\nThe compiler should slice exact 8 by 8 cells, deduplicate by pixel bytes plus semantic class, pack only into declared free atlas slots, and fail closed when the budget is impossible. It should emit normal project-consumable artifacts: PNG sprite or tileset assets, map section data, generated Lua modules for tile classes, decorative layer draw data, objects and anchors, plus a provenance and capacity report. Do not add a hard Paintop dependency. Exact Apollo64 is the default; any lossy mode must be explicit and produce error metrics and a heatmap.\n\nThe current runtime has one mutable map. The first implementation may emit decorative layers as deterministic Lua data rendered through a shared visible-cell helper. Treat named runtime map banks as a separately justified platform change if profiling shows that compiled draw lists are insufficient.\n\nAdd tileset lint and review output that catches unsupported neighbor masks, illegal edge pairs, orphan corners and endcaps, collision-class drift, overlapping anchors, blank or duplicate allocations, periodic variant cadence, and map or atlas overflow. Generate labeled atlas, live-shape, 3 by 3 repeat, used-adjacency, collision-overlay, and native map review sheets.\n\nCoordinate with existing bones rather than duplicating them: bn-2a5 owns semantic atlas reporting and live-map capture, bn-3k8 owns deterministic sequence and review-board capture, and bn-240 owns optional draw-event tracing. Their outputs should be composable with the compiler evidence.\n\n# Acceptance criteria\n- A checked-in example scene compiles from named visual layers and semantic data into byte-stable project assets and generated Lua modules.\n- Named metatiles, adjacency lookup, deterministic variants, manual overrides, collision classes, and object anchors all survive compile and are exercised by tests.\n- The same visual cell with different gameplay semantics cannot be accidentally merged into one semantic tile ID.\n- Atlas reservations, tile IDs, map bounds, object bounds, and all input paths are validated before atomic publication.\n- Exact mode performs no resizing, filtering, or implicit palette conversion and reports every used Apollo64 index.\n- Impossible exact packing fails with required versus available capacity and identifies the largest reuse opportunities; optional lossy packing is explicit and evidence-backed.\n- Review artifacts expose tile ownership, seams, legal adjacencies, repetition cadence, collision overlays, source-to-output provenance, and native-scale map composition.\n- A RIBBIT RECOIL environment subset replaces its shell and runtime substitution path with compiled artifacts and produces deterministic equivalent gameplay semantics.\n- Project build, run, and playtest consume generated artifacts without a special runtime dependency.\n- Help, specification, agent skill guidance, focused regressions, deterministic reruns, and full just check pass.","kind":"task","labels":["console-agent","project-compiler","map-tooling","visual-quality"],"parent":"bn-2lm","size":"xl","title":"Compile layered scenes, metatiles, and semantic tilemaps"}	blake3:_Vq3N0hilRpEdV-7tbq21TjUa9rFeQ_xSi6tvTdUs_E
