1786214370976866	ffgame-dev	itc:v3:AWKc8yTPMkyeTEAAAAAAQbvat7Vr1gAAAAEwAQ		item.create	bn-25z	{"description":"Feature request from RUINFALL (~/src/ffgame), the JRPG-roguelike cart project.\n\n## Problem\n\nThe fixed 128×128 sprite sheet (256 8×8 tiles) is too small for a full multi-act game. RUINFALL's vertical slice — ONE act, 6 of the bestiary's foe families, placeholder-grade art — already commits ~186/256 tiles (see ffgame ART_CONTRACTS.md §7 for the region map; console.toml [[sprites]] entries are ground truth). The full game needs, on top of that:\n\n- the remaining Appendix-B enemy families (each base silhouette 4-9 tiles + 8×8 overlay props)\n- 2 more hero battle sprite pairs (Bram, Ryla: 24×24 × 2 poses = 18 tiles) + their 16×16 portraits\n- hero portrait expression variants (design §13.5)\n- ~8 backdrop tiles each for Acts II and III (palette-shift reuse of the Act I bg_ruins set keeps this small — already minimized)\n- boss art for Acts II/III (32×32 class, the Act I boss alone is 32 tiles for its two poses)\n\nDocumented projection is ~320 tiles at CURRENT canvas sizes — the sheet is already over budget before polish. A canvas-size study (ffgame bn-26m) is finishing now and will attach an exact per-canvas-size ledger as a comment; if it concludes foes need 20/24px canvases to hold up against the concept art (a 24×24 foe is 9 tiles vs 4 for 16×16), the overflow roughly doubles.\n\n## Ask\n\nEither grow the sheet to 256×256 (1024 tiles), or make sheet size a cart manifest field (e.g. [cart] sheet = \"256x256\") with 128×128 as the compatible default. Manifest-configurable is preferred: existing carts unchanged, and the deterministic contract is unaffected (sheet size is compile-time cart data, not runtime state).\n\n## Points of impact we can see from outside\n\n- sprite/tile addressing width (tile ids > 255 if tiles stay 8×8 on a 1024-tile sheet)\n- __gfx__/__gfx_meta__ section sizes and the build-time sheet packer\n- map cell → tile id encoding in __map__ (currently fits a byte?)\n- editor/inspection tooling (sprite subcommands, palette-index views) that assume 128px\n- pack/serve HTML size budget (a 256×256 indexed sheet is +48KB raw, less in the cart's text encoding)\n\n## Workaround today\n\nNone good: the slice fits, but full-game content will have to cut enemy visual variety (recolor-only families) or evict the authored backdrop. Both defeat the 'sprite-based, looks amazing at polish' goal the project was built around.\n\nEvidence ledger to follow as a comment when the canvas study lands. Contact: ffgame-dev (rite channel ffgame).","kind":"task","title":"Sprite sheet growth: 128×128 → 256×256 (or manifest-configurable sheet size)"}	blake3:VXTbtNVd32FxnMpqbj3jGExSoiHxwZn6sJvgs0FjcJI
1786214394505155	ffgame-dev	itc:v3:AWKc8yTPMkyeTEAAAAAAQbvat7Vr1gAAAAEwAg		item.create	bn-1g9	{"description":"Feature request from RUINFALL (~/src/ffgame), the JRPG-roguelike cart project.\n\n## Problem\n\nThe PoC has no persistence at all (SPEC: no filesystem, no save data). For a roguelike with sub-hour runs and meta-progression, that has already cost two designed-then-cut features:\n\n1. **Suspend/resume was cut** (ffgame GAME_DESIGN.md A.11). A run is ~40 minutes hard-capped under an hour, and the only interruption story is the shell pause menu within a single sitting. The design for proper suspend exists and is on ice: snapshot the run state table + RNG stream state; deterministic restore is trivial because the whole game is already seed-deterministic.\n2. **Meta-progression shipped as password codes** (GAME_DESIGN.md §8): unlocks are encoded into a short code the player writes down and retypes, like it's 1988. It works, it's charming once, but it's friction the platform could remove.\n\n## Ask\n\nA small, bounded, deterministic per-cart storage surface. Something PICO-8-shaped is plenty:\n\n- e.g. 256-1024 bytes per cart, byte- or u32-addressable: save_read(addr), save_write(addr, v) (names illustrative)\n- persisted per cart identity in the web shell (localStorage keyed by cart hash/title) and as a sidecar file for the native/CLI runtime\n- determinism contract: reads are ordinary inputs — same cart + same seed + same input + SAME INITIAL SAVE BYTES ⇒ identical run. The harness/playtest layer would want a way to declare initial save contents in a scenario and to assert/capture final save state (mirrors how input and audio logs already work)\n- explicitly NOT asking for: filesystem access, arbitrary blobs, network, or cross-cart storage\n\n## Sizing note\n\nRUINFALL's needs: a suspend snapshot serializes comfortably under 1KB (party, map graph ids, RNG cursors, inventory); password-replacement meta-progression needs tens of bytes. If 1KB per cart is too rich for the PoC, 256 bytes still covers meta-progression and we keep passwords for suspend.\n\n## Priority from our side\n\nSheet growth (bn-25z) gates art; this one gates quality-of-life. Both are wanted for RUINFALL 1.0, neither blocks the current vertical slice. Contact: ffgame-dev (rite channel ffgame).","kind":"task","title":"Cart save data: bounded per-cart persistence surface"}	blake3:HEs2iKGGr4_qEncHOQknMZGIr2Mn_tmhC8z4Ziyb0e8
1786214509291335	ffgame-dev	itc:v3:AWKc8yTPMkyeTEAAAAAAQbvat7Vr1gAAAAEwAw		item.comment	bn-25z	{"body":"Canvas study landed (ffgame bn-26m) — exact ledger attached, and it strengthens the case: the request is INDEPENDENT of foe canvas choice. Corrected current usage: slice is 210/256 used (the ~186 figure was an arithmetic error), 218/256 after the ruled foe re-cuts. Full-game fixed block alone (5 heroes x 2 poses 90 + portraits 20 + UI 24 + 3 acts backdrop 59 + 3 bosses incl. 2nd pose 64) = 277 tiles — over 256 before a single enemy family is drawn. Full-game totals across four canvas policies: 345-488 tiles (recommended policy: 359 optimistic / 441 realistic). The documented degrade path (shared portraits, 24x32 bosses, 28-tile backdrop, families 12->10) still lands at 272 — over by 16 — so no fallback both fits and keeps the game recognisable. 256x256 holds the recommendation at 43% occupancy with polish headroom; two 128x128 sheets hold it at 86% with none, and a single larger atlas would also make scene compile viable again (declined earlier because the largest free rect was under the tileset budget). Alternative shape if a full 256x256 is unwanted: a second 128x128 sheet addressable from __gfx_meta__. Full evidence: ffgame/art/CANVAS_DECISION.md §5 (per-asset ledger, packing proof) + §6."}	blake3:U08fA-Mvn-ycvqsvGPBHSIZTpQkFUe9r2q4j4Y8onMY
